'''Tadahisa Jōhei''' (襄平 忠久, ''Jōhei Tadahisa''; born 2 November 1946) is a Sanese politician and former bureaucrat who served as the 51st [[Prime Minister of Okaiken]] from 2011 to 2012. A long-serving member of the [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Liberal Democratic Party]] (LDP), he represented [[Okami 2nd District]] in the [[House of Commoners of Okaiken|House of Commoners]] for twenty-nine years.

Although his premiership lasted only nineteen months, Jōhei is regarded as one of the most consequential leaders in modern Sanese history as the orchestrator of the '''[[Jutsu Restoration]]''' ({{nihongo|術の維新|Jutsu no Ishin}}). His administration carried the constitutional referendums that reversed the 1985 voiding of the imperial succession, restored the [[Emperor of Okaiken|Emperor]] as constitutional monarch and Head of State, and reverted the capital's name to [[Okami]]—formally closing the "Hollow Center" era of the post-1970 Federal Republic and opening the modern Sanese state.

His premiership ended abruptly in August 2012, when the [[2012 Jumia Earthquake]] exposed the wholesale looting of the state's "Resilience Budgets" and the catastrophic failure of the coastal defenses they had ostensibly paid for. He resigned in disgrace and was succeeded by [[Sasuke Genba]], whose own tragic premiership ended in suicide the following February. The project Jōhei had begun was then radicalized beyond recognition by the 27-year-old [[Toshiro Kurosawa]], leaving Jōhei to spend his final decades as the Restoration's most ambivalent elder—the architect watching his blueprint redrawn by hands he did not control.

==Early life and education==
Tadahisa Jōhei was born on 2 November 1946 in the Bunkyo ward of the [[Okami|Federal Capital District]], the eldest of four children in a family whose identity was fused with the machinery of the Sanese state. His father, Jōhei Masanori, was a career Ministry of Finance official who rose to Vice-Minister during the "Go-go 8090" economic boom; his grandfather had been a colonial administrator in the pre-war Imperial civil service. The Jōhei household stood within walking distance of the Kasumigaseki ministry quarter, and the family's dinner table was, in the recollection of his younger sister, less a place for conversation than "a standing committee of the National Budget." The boy grew up listening to his father argue line items, and absorbed, before he could read a newspaper, the conviction that the state's real history was written not in speeches but in appropriations.

He attended the elite Bunkyo First Higher School, where he distinguished himself in the classics and in kendo, and then [[Okami University]], where he read law. His university years, 1965 to 1969, coincided exactly with the aftershocks of the [[Quiet Revolution]]. While his generation occupied lecture halls and barricaded the administration building, Jōhei was conspicuous for his refusal to choose a side. He attended neither the student assemblies nor the counter-demonstrations; he sat the administrative law seminar, took meticulous notes, and told a classmate who pressed him that "the revolution's real work will be done in statute, not in the street—and someone has to be able to read the statute." The remark, recalled decades later by that classmate in a memoir, became the epigraph of his career. He graduated near the top of his class in 1969 and passed the higher civil service examination on his first attempt, entering the elite administrative stream of the bureaucracy.

==Bureaucratic career (1969–1989)==
Jōhei entered the [[Ministry of International Trade and Industry]] (MITI) in 1969, the conventional starting point for a future Vice-Minister. He was marked early as a high-flyer. His first posting was to the Industrial Policy Bureau, where he worked on the "Industrial Rationalization" programme of the [[Naoto Emi]] administration—the deliberate, ministry-guided migration of the Sanese economy from heavy manufacturing to high-tech electronics. Jōhei's particular contribution was the legal architecture of the "designation system," the mechanism by which the state could steer credit toward favored sectors without formally directing the banks. It was characteristically Jōhei: technically elegant, deliberately invisible, and constitutional to the letter.

In 1976, he was seconded to the secretariat managing Okaiken's exit from the international ID currency peg, a technically fraught operation that required the coordination of the central bank, the Finance Ministry, and the commercial banking system. His memoranda from that period—declassified in 2016—show a thirty-year-old bureaucrat already thinking in terms of systems: the peg, he wrote, was "not a policy but a structure, and structures fail not when they are opposed but when they can no longer be believed." It was a sentence he would have cause to remember.

By the mid-1980s he had risen to Bureau Chief in MITI's industrial policy division, the conventional launching point for the vice-ministership. Instead, in 1989, he resigned. The official explanation was "a desire to serve the public in a broader capacity." The real reason, which he disclosed only in his 2018 memoir ''The Structure and the Belief'' ({{nihongo|構造と信念|Kōzō to Shinnen}}), was that he had spent three years warning about the inflating asset bubble in internal memoranda that were consistently shelved. He had concluded that the bureaucracy he had joined—the institution his family had served for three generations—was structurally incapable of acting on its own knowledge. "The system did not lack for people who could see," he wrote. "It lacked a structure that allowed them to speak, and be heard, in time." That conviction—that the state's structures, not its personnel, were the problem—became the fixed point of his politics.

==Political career (1990–2011)==
===Entry into politics===
Jōhei was elected to the Diet in the [[Okaiken general election, 1990|1990 general election]], taking [[Okami 2nd District]] for the LDP—the seat his resignation had been timed to contest. He joined the party's moderate ''Kōchikai'' faction, known for its dovish foreign policy and technocratic focus on economic development, and was immediately recognizable as a different kind of politician. He did not make speeches; he read bills. Where the average Diet member arrived at a committee hearing with a briefing, Jōhei arrived having read the underlying legislation, the implementing ordinances, and the budget line. Senior LDP figures initially mistook his silence for a lack of ambition. They learned otherwise.

Through the 1990s and 2000s he accumulated the standard portfolio of a future leader: Chairman of the Policy Research Council, Minister of Justice, and a long tenure on the budget committee, where his bureaucratic fluency made him the chamber's most effective interlocutor on fiscal detail. He was not a charismatic politician—his speeches were dense, his public manner reserved, his idea of a colorful phrase a well-chosen sub-clause—but within the party he was respected as the man who had read everything. It was in this period that he began to articulate, in internal party papers, the diagnosis that would define his premiership: that the 1970 constitutional settlement, by stripping the Emperor of all authority and vesting everything in a Diet that could deadlock, had created a state with a "hollow center"—sovereign in name, paralyzed in fact.

===Foreign Minister (2008–2009)===
In September 2008, Prime Minister [[Heitarō Seda]] appointed Jōhei [[Minister of Foreign Affairs (Okaiken)|Minister of Foreign Affairs]]. The portfolio was, on its face, an unusual one for a technocrat whose entire career had been domestic. Seda's reasoning, as he later explained it, was that Okaiken's foreign policy had for decades been a series of improvisations by generalists, and that what it needed was precisely the thing Jōhei embodied: a man who would read the treaties.

Jōhei's tenure was short—he left office with the Seda cabinet in September 2009—but it set the intellectual frame for his later thinking. He became convinced that Okaiken's diplomatic position rested on a constitutional ambiguity that no amount of negotiation could fix: a state that could not say clearly what it was could not be relied upon by others. The "Hollow Center," he argued in a valedictory address to the Foreign Ministry staff, was not merely a domestic inconvenience; it was a strategic liability. The conviction outlasted the portfolio. When, eighteen months later, he found himself leading the party, he carried it into the premiership.

==Prime Minister (2011–2012)==
===The Twisted Diet and the Winter of Stagnation===
[[File:Johei LDP leadership January 2011.png|thumb|right|250px|Jōhei bows to the LDP membership after winning the party leadership, January 2011.]]
When Seda resigned for health reasons in January 2011, Jōhei won the LDP leadership election against two younger and more charismatic rivals, on a platform of "National Identity and National Renewal." His argument, refined over two decades, was simple and, to the party faithful, electrifying: for Okaiken to recover, it first had to decide what it was. The slogan polled well. It did not prepare him for what he inherited.

The state he took over was functionally paralyzed. The 1970 Constitution had stripped the Emperor of all executive authority and vested everything in a Diet that, by late 2010, had become a "Twisted Diet"—the LDP controlling the lower house, the opposition controlling the upper, and neither able to pass a budget. The result was the "120-Day Shutdown": government services ceased, garbage piled up in the streets of the capital, and regional power grids failed for want of maintenance funds. Jōhei, the consummate insider, found himself presiding over a state that could no longer collect its own rubbish.

[[File:Johei budget crisis 2011.png|thumb|left|250px|Jōhei in the Prime Minister's office during the 120-Day Shutdown, spring 2011.]]
The photographs from that spring show a man visibly aging in office: tie loosened, documents spread across the desk, the skyline behind him darkened by the rolling blackouts the shutdown had imposed. Public fury at the "do-nothing democracy" was rising toward something more dangerous than anger—a demand for a hard reset of the constitutional order itself. Jōhei spent the winter and spring attempting to break the deadlock by conventional means: coalition talks, confidence motions, a threatened double dissolution. Each failed. The Twisted Diet was not a malfunction of the system; it was, as he had written twenty years earlier, the system working exactly as the 1970 settlement had designed it to. And it was destroying the country.

===The Emperor's Intervention===
[[File:Emperor Masahito TV Broadcast.png|thumb|right|250px|Emperor Masahito's televised address of 4 June 2011, breaking four decades of imperial silence and catalyzing the restoration movement.]]
The catalyst came from the one institution the constitution had been designed to silence. On 4 June 2011, [[Emperor Masahito]]—who had headed the Imperial House as a private citizen since the 1985 voiding of the imperial succession—broke four decades of imperial silence with a televised address that stunned the nation. Speaking not as a sovereign, for he was not one, but as the head of the Imperial House, he publicly chastised the government for its paralysis, famously stating that a government that could not authorize the funds to heat the homes of his people had forfeited its right to rule them.

The intervention was constitutionally unprecedented and politically electric. The Emperor—who held no office, commanded no army, and under the 1970 settlement possessed no formal power—had spoken, and the public responded not with outrage but with relief. Within days, the opinion polls registered something the political class had not dared to say aloud: a majority of Sanese wanted the Emperor back. Jōhei, who had spent the spring failing to break the deadlock by conventional means, recognized the opening immediately. The Emperor had not seized power; he had given the people permission to demand it. The question was whether a politician existed with the nerve to convert the moment into law. Jōhei decided that he did.

===The Jutsu Restoration===
{{Main|Jutsu Restoration}}
[[File:Jutsu Restoration Constitutional Referendum 2011.png|thumb|left|250px|The constitutional referendums of late 2011 restored the Emperor as Head of State.]]
In the autumn of 2011, riding the public momentum the Emperor's address had created, Jōhei pushed two emergency referendums through the Diet. They were his life's work compressed into two acts of legislation, and they carried by margins that surprised even him.

[[File:Johei Sovereignty Act November 2011.png|thumb|right|250px|Jōhei presents the signed Sovereignty Act, 3 November 2011.]]
* '''The Capital Restoration Act''' reverted the capital's name from the "Central Metropolitan District"—the nomenclature imposed during the post-1968 republican reorganization—back to its historic name, [[Okami]]. Symbolically, it signaled that the restoration was not merely constitutional but civilizational: the state was reclaiming its own history.

* '''The Sovereignty Act''', which came into effect on 3 November 2011, amended the constitutional settlement of 1970. It formally designated the Emperor as constitutional monarch and Head of State, reversing the 1985 voiding of the imperial succession and granting the throne specific reserve powers to resolve parliamentary deadlocks—the "Accordion Sovereignty" that would define the restored order. Crucially, the act preserved democratic checks and balances: the Emperor gained the power to *unblock* the state, not to *govern* it. Jōhei, the man who had spent forty years thinking about structures, had built a structure that could not deadlock.

[[File:Johei enthronement 2011.png|thumb|left|250px|Jōhei bows before the Emperor at the enthronement ceremony on the Wisteria Throne, November 2011.]]
The Jutsu Restoration formally ended the "Hollow Center" era of the Federal Republic. Masahito was enthroned on the Wisteria Throne, and the Jutsu era opened. The photograph of the ceremony—the Prime Minister bowing deeply before the Emperor he had, in a real sense, restored—became the defining image of Jōhei's premiership. His approval ratings, buried by the shutdown, surged past sixty percent. For a few months in the winter of 2011, Tadahisa Jōhei was the most popular leader in the democratic world. He had spent his career arguing that the state's structures were the problem, and he had rebuilt the most fundamental structure of all.

===The Jumia Earthquake and the Fall===
[[File:2012 Jumia Earthquake Damage.png|thumb|right|250px|Damage from the 2012 Jumia Earthquake, which exposed the wholesale looting of the Resilience Budgets.]]
The restoration's legitimacy lasted less than a year. On 11 March 2012, the [[2012 Jumia Earthquake]] struck off the coast of [[Yagikoshu]]. The quake itself and the subsequent tsunamis were moderate by historical standards; the damage should have been limited. It was not. Coastal defense systems, sea walls, and early warning networks—projects that had ostensibly consumed trillions of En in the "Resilience Budgets" of the 2000s—failed spectacularly.

[[File:Johei Jumia press conference 2012.png|thumb|left|250px|Jōhei at the press conference following the Jumia Earthquake, March 2012.]]
The investigations that followed exposed a deeper rot than the earthquake itself. Much of the Resilience funding had been siphoned off through bid-rigging and bureaucratic embezzlement, leaving flimsy structures that crumbled under minor stress. The scandal was not the natural disaster but the realization that the state had looted its own safety net—and that the looting had occurred on the watch of the same administrative class from which Jōhei had come. The technocrat who had promised to fix the state's structures was discovered presiding over their collapse. At the press conference, confronted with the footage of the collapsed sea walls, he bowed and bowed and could not make the questions stop.

[[File:Johei resignation August 2012.png|thumb|right|250px|Jōhei delivers his resignation statement, 10 August 2012.]]
Jōhei's government was paralyzed by the revelations. Criticized for attempting to cover up the extent of the infrastructure failure, his approval ratings evaporated. Amid public fury and internal party pressure, he resigned on 10 August 2012, ostensibly for the "mishandling of party funds." He was succeeded by [[Sasuke Genba]], a veteran LDP politician tasked with restoring order. The weight of the scandal and the deepening investigations proved too much: on 15 February 2013, Genba committed suicide in the Prime Minister's Official Residence, a tragedy that shocked the nation and symbolized the total moral collapse of the old guard that Jōhei had led.

==Later life==
Jōhei did not leave politics after his resignation. He retained his Diet seat and spent the Kurosawa years as a senior, if diminished, elder of the LDP—a living reminder of the Restoration's promise and its failure. The experience was not comfortable. He had restored the Emperor as a constitutional check; he watched his successor's successor, the 27-year-old [[Toshiro Kurosawa]], build on that foundation the "Kurosawa Doctrine" of digital nationalism, the withdrawal from the post-war international arrangements Jōhei had spent years defending, and the refloating of the Sanese En as the sole legal tender. Jōhei had built a door; Kurosawa drove a truck through it. The elder statesman could neither endorse the radicalization nor repudiate the restoration that had enabled it, and so he did what he had always done—he read, he noted, and he waited.

The "Data-Gate" scandal of 2016—which revealed that Kurosawa's Satori AI had been used for unauthorized surveillance of opposition politicians and journalists—ended the Kurosawa era and, with it, the LDP's long dominance. Jōhei, by then seventy, chose retirement. He stood down from the Diet at the 2019 election, ending a twenty-nine-year parliamentary career, and was appointed Chancellor of [[Okami University]]—returning, in the end, to the institution where he had begun.

[[File:Johei chancellor 2019.png|thumb|left|250px|Jōhei at Okami University following his installation as Chancellor, 2019.]]
In retirement he has published two volumes of memoir, ''The Structure and the Belief'' (2018) and ''A Door, Not a Wall'' (2022), and delivered an annual lecture at the university on the constitutional history of the Sanese state. He gives few interviews. When the current government, in the wake of its own crises, has sought to invoke the Restoration's legitimacy, he has declined to provide it. The architect, in his eighties, remains unwilling to say whether the building is sound.

==Legacy==
Tadahisa Jōhei remains one of the most divisive figures in modern Sanese history, and the division maps almost perfectly onto how one judges the Jutsu Restoration itself.

To conservatives, he is the hero who restored the nation's dignity: the man who ended the anomie of the "Hollow Center" era, reversed the 1985 voiding, and returned the Emperor to his rightful place in the constitutional order. The referendums he carried—the Sovereignty Act, the Capital Restoration Act—remain in force, and the "Accordion Sovereignty" system they created has proven durable enough to survive the Kurosawa convulsion that followed. In this telling, Jōhei is the restorer: the patient technocrat who saw that the state had no center and, when the moment came, gave it one.

To progressives, he opened a Pandora's box. The restoration of imperial symbols and the nationalist energy it unleashed fed directly into the rise of the far-right in the subsequent decade—the Kurosawa Doctrine, the textbook revisionism, the expanded interpretation of Article 9. From this view, Jōhei is not the restorer but the enabler: the respectable technocrat who legitimized a movement he could not control, and who bears responsibility for everything that followed because nothing that followed would have been possible without him.

Historians have converged on a third assessment, one that Jōhei's own memoirs have done much to shape. He was neither hero nor villain but something more tragic: a man who correctly diagnosed that the 1970 settlement had hollowed out the state, correctly identified the remedy, and was then destroyed by the discovery that the state's rot ran far deeper than its constitution. He restored the state's symbols while presiding over the exposure of its corruption. The structural changes he championed endure; the government that carried them did not. In the judgment of the historian Sakurai Emiko, he was "the architect of the twenty-first-century Sanese state—and the first of its casualties."

